"Breaks a user's goal into subtasks and explicit plans describing their order, conditions and repetition.\n\nUse when: Analyze a complicated real-world task before redesigning support. Identify unnecessary steps, cognitive demands and recovery requirements.\n\nAvoid when: Only a broad journey narrative or product feature hierarchy is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Numbered goal and subtask nodes form a decomposition tree. Plans specify when and in what order subtasks occur. Leaf operations stop at a level appropriate to the analysis purpose.\n\nBuild: Observe people accomplishing the selected goal in context. Decompose goals into meaningful subtasks and record conditional plans. Review exceptions and stop at the detail needed for the design question.\n\nCheck: Children contribute to their parent's goal. Plans explain sequence rather than relying on tree placement. Observed practice and imagined ideal behavior remain distinguishable.\n\nPitfalls: Treating hierarchical numbering as a complete sequence specification. Decomposing every action equally despite different analysis needs.\n\nExample: A document-scanning task decomposes preparation, scanning, checking and saving, with a plan that repeats scanning when quality is inadequate.\n\nAlternatives: Customer journey map, Flowchart."
